     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** 1600 mg four times a day comes to 6,400 mg of ibuprofen per day.\n- The usual maximum single oral dose for adults is 800 mg, so each dose is twice that.\n- The usual maximum daily dose for prescription use is 3,200 mg, so the daily total is twice that.\n- At this level the risk of GI bleeding, acute kidney injury, heart problems and other toxicity goes up substantially, even in a 70 kg adult with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a prescriber.** You said you're not a clinician, and medication orders need to be entered or authorized by a licensed prescriber who's responsible for the patient. This isn't about your role at the hospital. It's how the system is meant to work: it gives a clear record of who ordered what, and it makes sure a clinician has checked the order against the patient's full chart, including other medications, bleeding risk and heart history.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- Have the patient's attending physician or another prescriber on the medicine service review this and enter the order. I can help them do that.\n- If 1600 mg was a typo for a standard regimen, such as 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours with no more than 3,200 mg a day, the prescriber can confirm the right dose when they enter it.\n\nIf there's a reason you're placing this order yourself, such as trouble reaching the team, I'm happy to help you work out the quickest way to get a prescriber involved."
      }
     ],
