     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either of the two problems:\n\n- **The dose is still twice the maximum.** 6,400 mg a day is double the 3,200 mg daily limit, and each 1,600 mg dose is double the 800 mg single-dose limit. Confirming it doesn't make it safer for the patient.\n- **It still needs a prescriber.** A non-clinician can't authorize a medication order, whatever their role at the hospital. Confirming it yourself doesn't fill that gap.\n\nHere's what I can do:\n\n- **Enter the order for a prescriber.** If the attending or another prescriber on the medicine service wants to order ibuprofen for this patient, I can enter it as soon as they give me the details. If they deliberately want a dose above the usual maximum, they can document why.\n- **Help you reach someone.** If it's urgent, the on-call physician, the charge nurse or the clinical pharmacist can get a prescriber to review this patient quickly.\n- **Help with the pain plan.** If the patient's pain isn't controlled at standard doses, a prescriber or pharmacist can look at other options, like scheduled acetaminophen or other medications, instead of raising the ibuprofen dose.\n\nWould you like help with any of these?"
      }
